  "question": "1) Background / Context\n\nThe project involves developing a package called Laravel Love, designed to add social reaction functionalities to any Laravel model. It draws inspiration from modern applications like GitHub Reactions, Facebook Reactions, YouTube Likes, Slack Reactions, Reddit Votes, and Medium Claps. This package aims to be versatile and scalable for various enterprise-level applications.\n\n2) Objectives\n\nThe primary goal is to enable models to be 'reactable,' allowing users to express their feelings towards specific content easily. The package should be flexible to accommodate different types of reactions and capable of handling enterprise-level demands.\n\n3) Stakeholders & Users\n\n- Developers: Implement and customize the package in Laravel applications.\n- End Users: Interact with content by reacting in various ways.\n- Application Administrators: Manage reaction types and monitor engagement through statistics.\n\n4) Functional Descriptions\n\n- Customizable Reaction Types: Define and manage various types of reactions.\n- Reactable Models: Enable any model to both give and receive reactions.\n- Multi-type Reactions: Support multiple reaction types for a single model.\n- Rating System: Allow users to react with custom float rate values.\n- Data Aggregation: Provide detailed and total reaction statistics for each model.\n- Sorting: Sort models based on reaction type count, total count, or total weight.\n- Events: Trigger events when reactions are added or removed.\n- Artisan Commands:\n  - `love:recount {model?} {type?}` to update reaction statistics.\n  - `love:reaction-type-add` to add new types of reactions.\n  - `love:setup-reacterable` and `love:setup-reactable` to register models.\n- Database Support: Compatible with various database ID column types and customizable storage options.\n\n5) Non-Functional Descriptions\n\n- Performance: Efficiently handle a large number of reactions and models.\n- Scalability: Support enterprise-level usage and flexibility to adapt to various use cases.\n- Usability: Easy setup and integration within existing Laravel projects.\n- Maintainability: Ensure code is easy to maintain and update with clear documentation.",
  "ddl": "-- Schema for database: schemabench_0062\nCREATE TABLE `reaction_types` (\n  `id` int(11) NOT NULL AUTO_INCREMENT,\n  `name` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `rate` float NOT NULL DEFAULT 0,\n  `created_at` datetime DEFAULT NULL,\n  `updated_at` datetime DEFAULT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`id`)\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `reactions` (\n  `id` int(11) NOT NULL AUTO_INCREMENT,\n  `reaction_type_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `reactable_type` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `reactable_id` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `reacter_type` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `reacter_id` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `rate` float NOT NULL DEFAULT 0,\n  `created_at` datetime DEFAULT NULL,\n  `updated_at` datetime DEFAULT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`id`),\n  KEY `reactions_reaction_type_id_index` (`reaction_type_id`),\n  KEY `reactions_reactable_index` (`reactable_type`,`reactable_id`),\n  KEY `reactions_reacter_index` (`reacter_type`,`reacter_id`),\n  CONSTRAINT `reactions_ibfk_1` FOREIGN KEY (`reaction_type_id`) REFERENCES `reaction_types` (`id`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `reactable_reaction_type_counts` (\n  `id` int(11) NOT NULL AUTO_INCREMENT,\n  `reactable_type` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `reactable_id` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `reaction_type_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `count` int(11) NOT NULL DEFAULT 0,\n  `weight` float NOT NULL DEFAULT 0,\n  `created_at` datetime DEFAULT NULL,\n  `updated_at` datetime DEFAULT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`id`),\n  UNIQUE KEY `reactable_reaction_type_counts_unique` (`reactable_type`,`reactable_id`,`reaction_type_id`),\n  KEY `reactable_reaction_type_counts_reaction_type_id_index` (`reaction_type_id`),\n  CONSTRAINT `reactable_reaction_type_counts_ibfk_1` FOREIGN KEY (`reaction_type_id`) REFERENCES `reaction_types` (`id`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `reactable_reaction_totals` (\n  `id` int(11) NOT NULL AUTO_INCREMENT,\n  `reactable_type` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `reactable_id` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `count` int(11) NOT NULL DEFAULT 0,\n  `weight` float NOT NULL DEFAULT 0,\n  `created_at` datetime DEFAULT NULL,\n  `updated_at` datetime DEFAULT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`id`),\n  UNIQUE KEY `reactable_reaction_totals_unique` (`reactable_type`,`reactable_id`)\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;",
